<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wade in the Water</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words first published <a href="/years/1901">1901</a>; first commercial recording <a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Wade in the Water&quot; is an African American spiritual. Its words were first printed in 1901 in New Jubilee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> as Sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers, published by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wesley">John Wesley</a> Work Jr. and his brother Frederick J. Work. Work, also known as John Work II (1871–1925), spent three decades at Fisk University, the historically black college in Nashville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, collecting the songs of the original Fisk Jubilee Singers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The song belongs to the tradition carried by the Fisk Jubilee Singers, an a cappella student chorus of Fisk University that sang from 1871 to 1878 and introduced spirituals to a wider audience. The chorus first toured to raise money for the university, setting out on 6 October 1871, and by 1873 had sung at the White House and toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Ella Sheppard, one of its original members, recalled that the slave songs were at first kept out of its programmes because they were sacred to the singers' parents, who used them in worship.</p><p>After the original group disbanded, Sheppard returned to Fisk in 1890 to coach new singers, among them John Work II. His male quartet later made a series of recordings for Victor, Edison, Columbia and Starr between 1909 and 1916. The first commercial recording of the song itself, by the Sunset Four Jubilee Singers, was issued by Paramount Records in 1925.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The printed words open with the call to wade in the water, children, followed by the line that God's gonna trouble the water. Later collections varied the text: James W. Johnson's 1926 Book of American Negro Spirituals titles it God's A-gwineter Trouble De Water, and a 1930s version from Sumter County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, and a 1956 version from Kentucky and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> open with other lines. Some interpreters read the song as recalling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>' escape from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in Exodus. Others, among them the theologian Howard Thurman, trace the troubled water to the pool of Bethesda in the fifth chapter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a>, where an angel was said to stir the water that healed the sick.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The song is also linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>. It is believed, though not proven, that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman">Harriet Tubman</a> used it to tell people escaping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> to leave the trail for the water, so that the slave-catchers' dogs would lose their scent. The musicologist Arthur C. Jones described how Tubman and others gave existing spirituals a hidden meaning in the struggle for freedom. In the 20th century the Staple Singers' version became part of the civil rights movement, the Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater set part of its ballet Revelations to it in 1960, and the Ramsey Lewis Trio's instrumental of 1966 reached number 19 on the Hot 100.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wade in the Water” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Steal Away&quot; is counted, with &quot;Swing Low, Sweet Chariot&quot;, &quot;<b>Wade in the Water</b>&quot; and &quot;The Gospel Train&quot;, among the spirituals said to carry hidden codes: besides their faith in God, messages telling enslaved people to run away, on their own or by the Underground Railroad.</blockquote><a href="/songs/steal-away">Steal Away</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Wade in the Water</b>&quot; is an African American spiritual.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a></li></ul></section>
